les in `B` and `A` of length `k · 9^s`. By\npigeonhole `k` of the pairs have the same pair of atomic types over\n`p̄`, so on them `H` agrees with `G` or with its complement, and this\nyields `N` such that the tuple `w̄` from `A` and the tuple `ū` from `B`\nsatisfy `N(wⱼ, uⱼ)` and `¬N(wⱼ', uⱼ)` for `j < j'`. Then `p̄ v` and `ū`\nextend the chosen pattern with base `w̄` by a row, as `ū` lies in `B`\nand the old grid points do not, contradicting maximality.\n\nThe argument is carried out for the graph classes of the archive and\ntheir monadic dependence, and transferred to the graph classes used\nhere.","nodeId":"p:Lax185574Proofs.BallTypes.ball_types","semanticId":"Lax185574Proofs.BallTypes.ball_types","tooltipHtml":"The proof in the paper. For each symmetric <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⊆</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π</mi><mi>s</mi></msup><mo>×</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Π</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R ⊆ Π^s × Π^s</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.8193em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0077em;\">R</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">⊆</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"katex-base\"><span class=\"katex-strut\" style=\"height:0.7667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\">Π</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"katex-sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal 